ol_overview.png; paper.tex references figure missing from figure_manifest.json: figures/main_results.pdf\n\n## Supplementary Information validation\n- appendix_present: `True`\n- heading_present: `True`\n- placed_after_references: `True`\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\n- clearpage_present: `True`\n- visible_character_count: `5932`\n- substantive_element_count: `9`\n- rendered_checked: `False`\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\n\n## Evidence validation\n- bundle_path: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/scientific-evidence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `52`\n- resolved_references: `17`\n- anchored_references: `35`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `3`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n- summary: The reported numbers match the evidence trail, but the paper presents the sealed-evaluated implementation more firmly than the evidence supports, and one hypothesis verdict is inconsistent with the paper's own caveats.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `sealed-config-identity-1` (Method (Compared approaches), Discussion (Threats to validity 4), Abstract/Conclusion): The evidence does not establish that the sealed evaluation ran the described proposed pair. The development scores of the proposed pair and the latent-extension pair tie exactly, and the paper's own Supplementary Information says the evaluated source-tree hash could not be matched to a single source directory. The main text still states the identity of the frozen configuration as fact and says \"evaluated once\", which no artifact records. The abstract and conclusion attribute the sealed numbers to the stack described in the Method.\n  - claim: Proposed pair (frozen for the sealed evaluation); the pair was chosen on the development partition and then evaluated once on the sealed partitions\n  - evidence: paper_data.json final_evaluation.program_tree_sha256 (sha256:3f76de56...) is not reproduced by sha256sum of source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py (62f9cd0a...) or config_latent_ext/program.py (d68d9520...); analysis/search_metrics.json entries 02 and 03 have identical metrics and seed scores; paper.tex Supplementary Information says the hash could not be matched; paper_data.json evaluation_evidence has candidate_diagnostics_trust=unverified.\n  - requested_fix: State in the main text (Compared approaches and Method) that the sealed run used either the proposed pair or its latent-skill extension, and that the available evidence cannot say which. Remove or qualify \"frozen for the sealed evaluation\" and \"evaluated once\". Make the abstract and conclusion refer to the evaluated selector-imputer family rather than asserting the exact described pair.\n- `si-hyperparameters-source-1` (Supplementary Information (Method specification and hyperparameters)): The constants were read from the config_proposed copy, whose match to the sealed-evaluated configuration is unverified. The same section says the evaluated hash does not match any source directory. The table is therefore presented as the evaluated configuration's constants without the needed qualification.\n  - claim: Table tab:hyper constants are read from the entry-point source of the evaluated configuration (src/program.py)\n  - evidence: source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py holds the table values (EM_SHRINK=0.02, STACK_TAUS, CANDIDATES, budgets 70/150/210). config_latent_ext/program.py is a different file and the tree hash is unmatched, per the paper_data.json final_evaluation record.\n  - requested_fix: Reword the table caption and lead-in to say the constants come from the proposed-pair source copy, and that this copy is not verified as the exact sealed-evaluated tree.\n- `h3-verdict-inconsistent-1` (Introduction (What the evidence supports) vs Results and Discussion): The Introduction counts the 0.016 margin as weak support for H3 (selector adds accuracy beyond random selection). The Results section says the sealed metrics do not isolate selection and that H3 is \"not tested sharply\". The Discussion says the margin should be viewed as indistinguishable from zero and makes no significance claim. The only retained selection-only evidence points the other way: skill against greedy ridge exceeds skill against random-k ridge, so the greedy baseline did no better than random.\n  - claim: H3 is supported only weakly (a 0.016 skill margin over random-k ridge)\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claim on skill 0.099 vs 0.016; paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics.final_skill_vs_random_selection=0.0160; paper.tex Results (Selection versus imputation; What a different outcome would have looked like) and Discussion (Uncertainty).\n  - requested_fix: Change the Introduction to say H3 is not tested by the sealed evaluation, because selector and imputer are scored jointly. Report the 0.016 margin as small and unresolved, consistent with the Results and Discussion.\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft is well supported overall: the methods (EM, stacking, NNLS, submodular selection, ridge), item-level cheaper-evaluation work, and latent-capability work are all cited, and the quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`. Three targeted gaps remain. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **related-work-comparators** (Related Work, high, comparator)\n   - Anchor: \"Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion ... are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices\"; \"here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark\".\n   - The closest prior work predicts unrun benchmark scores from a few observed ones, applies low-rank or matrix-completion methods to LLM score matrices, or selects what to run adaptively. The draft cites only generic matrix-completion sources and item-level work. The paper's differentiation claim is therefore not positioned against its direct competitors.\n   - Search topics: predicting LLM performance on unseen benchmarks from a benchmark subset; matrix completion or low-rank factorization of model-by-benchmark matrices; adaptive or IRT-based active selection for LLM evaluation.\n\n2. **intro-benchmark-redundancy-claim** (Introduction, medium, positioning)\n   - Anchor: \"are known to overlap in what they measure\".\n   - The premise that motivates budgeted evaluation is cited only to the suites themselves, which do not document redundancy.\n   - Search topics: correlation or redundancy among LLM benchmarks; low-dimensional structure in benchmark scores.\n\n3. **data-open-llm-leaderboard-context** (Method, protocol data paragraph, medium, benchmark_context)\n   - Anchor: \"the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models\".\n   - The leaderboard is one of three sealed evaluation datasets, and its six component benchmarks are named without citations. HELM and MTEB are cited.\n   - Search topics: Open LLM Leaderboard v2 description; IFEval, BBH, GPQA, MuSR and MMLU-Pro papers.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Cross-benchmark score prediction and matrix completion on LLM evaluation matrices\n- Adaptive or IRT-based test and benchmark selection for LLM evaluation\n- Open LLM Leaderboard and its component benchmarks\n- Empirical evidence of benchmark redundancy\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- Rank-Gauss transform and kNN: standard components, and the draft already states them as such.\n- Numeric results and protocol facts: grounded in evidence files.\n- Limitations and discussion text: deliberately self-critical, with no external claims that need support.\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52aaa56e-e9a6-4eb1-bcf5-1fba4e8e3ca8","uuid":"2204ecf2-9f70-491a-8449-82e5dd0f8118","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:00:59.475Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"VENUE.md\nanalysis\nclaims_grounding.json\nconcept_figure_run.log\ndraft_reference_gaps.json\ndraft_reference_gaps.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log\ndraft_reference_gaps_prompt.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.json\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.md\nfigure_manifest.json\nfigures\nglobal_memory\nglobal_memory_recent.json\nglobal_memory_summary.md\nmemory.md\npaper.aux\npaper.bbl\npaper.blg\npaper.bst\npaper.fls\npaper.log\npaper.out\npaper.sty\npaper.tex\npaper_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log\npaper_data.json\npaper_diagnostics\npaper_diagnostics_20261111_122649_37076.log\npaper_events_20261111_122649.jsonl\npaper_evidence_audit.json\npaper_evidence_audit.md\npaper_evidence_aud