{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Au6AiBTv4RUsWP3uxqwpWp","type":"tool_result","content":"# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T13:00:50.860025+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n- audit_json_path: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/paper_evidence_audit.json`\n- audit_markdown_path: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md`\n- raw_output_path: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/paper_evidence_audit_raw.txt`\n\n## Summary\nThe reported numbers match the evidence trail, but the paper presents the sealed-evaluated implementation more firmly than the evidence supports, and one hypothesis verdict is inconsistent with the paper's own caveats.\n\n## Findings\n- `sealed-config-identity-1` (Method (Compared approaches), Discussion (Threats to validity 4), Abstract/Conclusion): The evidence does not establish that the sealed evaluation ran the described proposed pair. The development scores of the proposed pair and the latent-extension pair tie exactly, and the paper's own Supplementary Information says the evaluated source-tree hash could not be matched to a single source directory. The main text still states the identity of the frozen configuration as fact and says \"evaluated once\", which no artifact records. The abstract and conclusion attribute the sealed numbers to the stack described in the Method.\n  - claim: Proposed pair (frozen for the sealed evaluation); the pair was chosen on the development partition and then evaluated once on the sealed partitions\n  - evidence: paper_data.json final_evaluation.program_tree_sha256 (sha256:3f76de56...) is not reproduced by sha256sum of source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py (62f9cd0a...) or config_latent_ext/program.py (d68d9520...); analysis/search_metrics.json entries 02 and 03 have identical metrics and seed scores; paper.tex Supplementary Information says the hash could not be matched; paper_data.json evaluation_evidence has candidate_diagnostics_trust=unverified.\n  - requested_fix: State in the main text (Compared approaches and Method) that the sealed run used either the proposed pair or its latent-skill extension, and that the available evidence cannot say which. Remove or qualify \"frozen for the sealed evaluation\" and \"evaluated once\". Make the abstract and conclusion refer to the evaluated selector-imputer family rather than asserting the exact described pair.\n- `si-hyperparameters-source-1` (Supplementary Information (Method specification and hyperparameters)): The constants were read from the config_proposed copy, whose match to the sealed-evaluated configuration is unverified. The same section says the evaluated hash does not match any source directory. The table is therefore presented as the evaluated configuration's constants without the needed qualification.\n  - claim: Table tab:hyper constants are read from the entry-point source of the evaluated configuration (src/program.py)\n  - evidence: source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py holds the table values (EM_SHRINK=0.02, STACK_TAUS, CANDIDATES, budgets 70/150/210). config_latent_ext/program.py is a different file and the tree hash is unmatched, per the paper_data.json final_evaluation record.\n  - requested_fix: Reword the table caption and lead-in to say the constants come from the proposed-pair source copy, and that this copy is not verified as the exact sealed-evaluated tree.\n- `h3-verdict-inconsistent-1` (Introduction (What the evidence supports) vs Results and Discussion): The Introduction counts the 0.016 margin as weak support for H3 (selector adds accuracy beyond random selection). The Results section says the sealed metrics do not isolate selection and that H3 is \"not tested sharply\". The Discussion says the margin should be viewed as indistinguishable from zero and makes no significance claim. The only retained selection-only evidence points the other way: skill against greedy ridge exceeds skill against random-k ridge, so the greedy baseline did no better than random.\n  - claim: H3 is supported only weakly (a 0.016 skill margin over random-k ridge)\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claim on skill 0.099 vs 0.016; paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics.final_skill_vs_random_selection=0.0160; paper.tex Results (Selection versus imputation; What a different outcome would have looked like) and Discussion (Uncertainty).\n  - requested_fix: Change the Introduction to say H3 is not tested by the sealed evaluation, because selector and imputer are scored jointly. Report the 0.016 margin as small and unresolved, consistent with the Results and Discussion.\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 7.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"protocol_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/protocol_overview.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/protocol_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"source_ids\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        4,\n        4,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 6.6,\n      \"figure_id\": \"main_results\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 8,\n        \"legend\": 7.2,\n        \"ticks\": 7.5,\n        \"title\": 8\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"n_panels\": 3,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/main_results.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/main_results.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1,\n        1,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_helm\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_oll\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k2\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k5\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k10\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k20\",\n        \"final.metric.final_score\",\n        \"final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge\",\n        \"final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\nmain_results.pdf\nmain_results.pdf.prov.json\nmain_results.png\nmain_results.png.prov.json\nprotocol_overview.png\nprotocol_overview.png.prov.json\nprotocol_overview.prompts.json\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"How Far Do k Benchmarks Go? Cold-Start Benchmark Selection and Score Imputation on Family-Disjoint Held-Out Models\",\n  \"central_question\": \"When only k benchmarks can be run on a new model, how accurately can a history-fitted selector-plus-imputer recover its scores on the remaining benchmarks, how does that error fall with k, and how much of the gain comes from cross-benchmark structure versus the choice of which k to run?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On three public leaderboards (MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard, HELM Lite) with family-disjoint held-out models, a wrapper-selected, rank-Gauss-transformed, NNLS-stacked Gaussian imputer cuts held-out error substantially relative to column-mean imputation (final qualified outcome; skill 0.434, nRMSE 0.562) and beats permuted-observation controls by a wide margin, but its edge over random-k selection with a ridge imputer is small (0.016), error falls only with diminishing returns in k (MTEB nRMSE 0.787 at k=2 to 0.638 at k=20), and difficulty differs by leaderboard.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The sealed final evaluation completed with outcome qualified (final.qualification.outcome = qualified, status = completed, correct = true), so the paper can report measured results as a positive-but-modest finding. The strongest defensible story is a calibrated empirical characterization of the cold-start budget question: (i) cross-benchmark structure is real and exploitable (large gain over column mean, large permuted-control gap), (ii) the marginal value of choosing the subset cleverly versus randomly is small in this regime, and (iii) error vs k shows diminishing returns on MTEB. This answers the task's 'how large must k be' question with an error-vs-k curve rather than a threshold, and avoids overclaiming selection or a mechanism: the evaluator reports selector+imputer jointly, no ablation separating selection from imputation produced numbers (ablation_studies is empty, offline experiment scripts were not executed in the evidence), no theory check results exist, and candidate diagnostics are marked unverified. Thesis-first organization (question, protocol, method, error-vs-k, baselines/controls, per-leaderboard difficulty, limitations) beats discovery order. No downstream_study block exists in paper_data.json; the search stage (three model variants, all scored) is used only as a small, clearly labelled search-partition comparison on the MTEB search split, kept separate from the sealed final results.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not in edit mode: no previous_paper or staged inputs exist. Fresh framing; nothing from a prior manuscript is preserved or rejected beyond this default.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged paper, program, or edit context is present; framing is derived only from paper_data.json (final_evaluation, search-stage program records), the task contract/evaluators, and the candidate source files.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Wrapper-selected, rank-Gauss stacked Gaussian imputer (selected configuration)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The studied method (best search score; best_correct = c02). Components: rank-Gauss or z-score representation, EM Gaussian with missing entries, benchmark selection from covariance-trace greedy and K-fold held-out-error wrapper forward selection (choice by history-only CV), per-target NNLS stack of Gaussian conditional means at several shrinkages, ridge on selected benchmarks, kNN over history models and column median, range clipping, median-shrink fallback. Described from the source file; every tuned quantity uses history rows only.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Greedy conditional-variance selection with Gaussian conditional-mean imputation\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"First model variant; the only grounded comparison to the selected one under the search protocol (search nRMSE 1.067 vs 1.028; skill vs random 0.100 vs 0.132; permuted gap 0.522 vs 0.568). Differences confound transform, selector, stacking and clipping, so it must be presented as a variant comparison, not a component ablation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Monotone latent-skill member plus A-optimal latent selector added to the stack\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Third variant; its search metrics and per-seed scores are identical to the selected one (combined 0.2467, both seeds), i.e. no measurable gain. Report as a null result. Cause (member not retained by CV, vs. no effect) is unknown from the artifacts; state that.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evaluator baselines and controls: column-mean imputation, random-k ridge, greedy-forward-selection ridge, permuted and constant-observation controls\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"These define skill (1 - candidate nRMSE / baseline nRMSE, clipped to [-1,1]) and the permuted-control gap; they ground the 0.434 / 0.016 / 0.099 / 0.678 numbers. Only the aggregated values are available, not per-baseline nRMSE.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Error-vs-k on MTEB (final): nRMSE 0.787 (k=2), 0.722 (k=5), 0.664 (k=10), 0.638 (k=20); monotone with 