rationale\": \"Three compact panels in one row at full text width keep tick labels at >=8 pt; at column-fraction width the panels would be unreadable and the template has no two-column layout to rescue them. Export PDF plus 300-DPI PNG from source; reference the PDF in LaTeX.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 3,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 4,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1\n  298  5740 44030 paper.tex\n# Post-draft reference query plan\n\n**Status:** ready. The gap artifact reports three targeted gaps, so this plan has six queries and no broader refresh. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n**Central topic:** choosing k benchmarks to run on a new model and imputing its scores on the remaining benchmarks from a historical model-by-benchmark matrix (MTEB, HELM Lite, Open LLM Leaderboard).\n\n## Existing bibliography (not re-searched)\nThe bibliography already covers tinyBenchmarks, metabench, anchor points, efficient benchmarking, Sloth, observational scaling laws and BenchBench. It also covers EM, stacking, NNLS, ridge, submodular selection, Bayesian design, IRT (Embretson, Vania), MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB. None of these directions is repeated below.\n\n## Queries\n\n### Gap 1: related-work-comparators (high priority)\nRelated Work claims \"the unit of selection is a whole benchmark\" but is not positioned against benchmark-level competitors. The three queries cover the three directions named in the gap memo.\n1. **baseline_comparator:** \"predicting language model performance on unseen benchmarks from scores on a subset of benchmarks\". This targets direct competitors that predict unrun benchmark scores from a few observed ones.\n2. **baseline_comparator:** \"matrix completion low-rank factorization of model-by-benchmark score matrix for LLM evaluation\". The draft cites only generic matrix-completion sources (Candes, Salakhutdinov), so this looks for applications to LLM score matrices.\n3. **baseline_comparator:** \"adaptive testing item response theory active selection of evaluations for large language models\". This targets adaptive or IRT-based selection of what to run, beyond the textbook and item-reduction work already cited.\n\n### Gap 2: intro-benchmark-redundancy-claim (medium)\n4. **result_driven_theme:** \"redundancy and correlation among LLM benchmarks low-dimensional structure of benchmark scores\". The claim that suites \"are known to overlap in what they measure\" is cited only to the suites. This looks for empirical redundancy or correlation analyses beyond BenchBench and Observational Scaling Laws, which are already cited.\n\n### Gap 3: data-open-llm-leaderboard-context (medium)\n5. **benchmark_context:** \"Open LLM Leaderboard v2 evaluation of open language models\". This finds a source for one of the three sealed evaluation datasets.\n6. **benchmark_context:** \"IFEval instruction-following, BIG-Bench Hard, GPQA, MuSR, MMLU-Pro benchmark\". This finds sources for the six component benchmarks named in the protocol and appendix. BBH may already be partly covered by BIG-bench, and MATH Level 5 is not queried because the gap memo does not list it as a search topic. The retrieval step can resolve the individual papers.\n\n## Exclusions\nThe gap memo deliberately does not flag the rank-Gauss transform, kNN, numeric results, protocol facts, or the limitations and discussion text. No queries are planned for these. Queries must not name or hint at the prior published paper on this exact task.\n8d7\n< \n16d14\n< \n23a22,27\npostdraft_references.bib:27\nreferences.pre_postdraft.bib:27\nreferences.bib:30","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52aaa56e-e9a6-4eb1-bcf5-1fba4e8e3ca8","uuid":"39d39dab-be78-48ce-84d9-c5107c40da90","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:01:02.208Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T13:00:50.860025+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n- audit_json_path: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/paper_evidence_audit.json`\n- audit_markdown_path: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md`\n- raw_output_path: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/paper_evidence_audit_raw.txt`\n\n## Summary\nThe reported numbers match the evidence trail, but the paper presents the sealed-evaluated implementation more firmly than the evidence supports, and one hypothesis verdict is inconsistent with the paper's own caveats.\n\n## Findings\n- `sealed-config-identity-1` (Method (Compared approaches), Discussion (Threats to validity 4), Abstract/Conclusion): The evidence does not establish that the sealed evaluation ran the described proposed pair. The development scores of the proposed pair and the latent-extension pair tie exactly, and the paper's own Supplementary Information says the evaluated source-tree hash could not be matched to a single source directory. The main text still states the identity of the frozen configuration as fact and says \"evaluated once\", which no artifact records. The abstract and conclusion attribute the sealed numbers to the stack described in the Method.\n  - claim: Proposed pair (frozen for the sealed evaluation); the pair was chosen on the development partition and then evaluated once on the sealed partitions\n  - evidence: paper_data.json final_evaluation.program_tree_sha256 (sha256:3f76de56...) is not reproduced by sha256sum of source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py (62f9cd0a...) or config_latent_ext/program.py (d68d9520...); analysis/search_metrics.json entries 02 and 03 have identical metrics and seed scores; paper.tex Supplementary Information says the hash could not be matched; paper_data.json evaluation_evidence has candidate_diagnostics_trust=unverified.\n  - requested_fix: State in the main text (Compared approaches and Method) that the sealed run used either the proposed pair or its latent-skill extension, and that the available evidence cannot say which. Remove or qualify \"frozen for the sealed evaluation\" and \"evaluated once\". Make the abstract and conclusion refer to the evaluated selector-imputer family rather than asserting the exact described pair.\n- `si-hyperparameters-source-1` (Supplementary Information (Method specification and hyperparameters)): The constants were read from the config_proposed copy, whose match to the sealed-evaluated configuration is unverified. The same section says the evaluated hash does not match any source directory. The table is therefore presented as the evaluated configuration's constants without the needed qualification.\n  - claim: Table tab:hyper constants are read from the entry-point source of the evaluated configuration (src/program.py)\n  - evidence: source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py holds the table values (EM_SHRINK=0.02, STACK_TAUS, CANDIDATES, budgets 70/150/210). config_latent_ext/program.py is a different file and the tree hash is unmatched, per the paper_data.json final_evaluation record.\n  - requested_fix: Reword the table caption and lead-in to say the constants come from the proposed-pair source copy, and that this copy is not verified as the exact sealed-evaluated tree.\n- `h3-verdict-inconsistent-1` (Introduction (What the evidence supports) vs Results and Discussion): The Introduction counts the 0.016 margin as weak support for H3 (selector adds accuracy beyond random selection). The Results section says the sealed metrics do not isolate selection and that H3 is \"not tested sharply\". The Discussion says the margin should be viewed as indistinguishable from zero and makes no significance claim. The only retained selection-only evidence points the other way: skill against greedy ridge exceeds skill against random-k ridge, so the greedy baseline did no better than random.\n  - claim: H3 is supported only weakly (a 0.016 skill margin over random-k ridge)\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claim on skill 0.099 vs 0.016; paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics.final_skill_vs_random_selection=0.0160; paper.tex Results (Selection versus imputation; What a different outcome would have looked like) and Discussion (Uncertainty).\n  - requested_fix: Change the Introduction to say H3 is not tested by the sealed evaluation, because selector and imputer are scored jointly. Report the 0.016 margin as small and unresolved, consistent with the Results and Discussion.\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 7.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"protocol_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/protocol_overview.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/protocol_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"source_ids\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        4,\n        4,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 6.6,\n      \"figure_id\": \"main_results\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 8,\n        \"legend\": 7.2,\n        \"ticks\": 7.5,\n        \"title\": 8\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"n_panels\": 3,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/main_results.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/main_results.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1,\n        1,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_helm\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_oll\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k2\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k5\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k10\",\n        \"final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k20\",\n        \"final.metric.final_score\",\