mall in this regime, and (iii) error vs k shows diminishing returns on MTEB. This answers the task's 'how large must k be' question with an error-vs-k curve rather than a threshold, and avoids overclaiming selection or a mechanism: the evaluator reports selector+imputer jointly, no ablation separating selection from imputation produced numbers (ablation_studies is empty, offline experiment scripts were not executed in the evidence), no theory check results exist, and candidate diagnostics are marked unverified. Thesis-first organization (question, protocol, method, error-vs-k, baselines/controls, per-leaderboard difficulty, limitations) beats discovery order. No downstream_study block exists in paper_data.json; the search stage (three model variants, all scored) is used only as a small, clearly labelled search-partition comparison on the MTEB search split, kept separate from the sealed final results.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not in edit mode: no previous_paper or staged inputs exist. Fresh framing; nothing from a prior manuscript is preserved or rejected beyond this default.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged paper, program, or edit context is present; framing is derived only from paper_data.json (final_evaluation, search-stage program records), the task contract/evaluators, and the candidate source files.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Wrapper-selected, rank-Gauss stacked Gaussian imputer (selected configuration)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The studied method (best search score; best_correct = c02). Components: rank-Gauss or z-score representation, EM Gaussian with missing entries, benchmark selection from covariance-trace greedy and K-fold held-out-error wrapper forward selection (choice by history-only CV), per-target NNLS stack of Gaussian conditional means at several shrinkages, ridge on selected benchmarks, kNN over history mode