{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwBdnZPanfGfMHinkJnf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AKgEnYbvNe7suNiDghBV6s","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\np='paper.tex'; t=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global t\n    assert t.count(a)==1,a[:60]\n    t=t.replace(a,b)\n\n# abstract\nrep(\"We evaluate a selector--imputer pair that combines a rank-Gauss transform, an EM-fitted Gaussian with missing data, cross-validated wrapper selection, and a per-target non-negative stack of imputers.\",\n\"We evaluate a family of selector--imputer pairs built from a rank-Gauss transform, an EM-fitted Gaussian with missing data, cross-validated wrapper selection, and a per-target non-negative stack of imputers; the sealed run used one member of this family (the described pair or its latent-skill extension, which the available evidence cannot tell apart).\")\nrep(\"The pair attains pooled normalized RMSE 0.562\",\"The evaluated pair attains pooled normalized RMSE 0.562\")\n# intro H3\nrep(\"H4 is observed but confounded by the number of benchmarks per leaderboard, and H3 is supported only weakly (a 0.016 skill margin over random-$k$ ridge).\",\n\"H4 is observed but confounded by the number of benchmarks per leaderboard, and H3 is not tested by the sealed evaluation, because selector and imputer are scored jointly; the 0.016 skill margin over random-$k$ ridge is small and unresolved.\")\nrep(\"(ii) A reimplementable selector--imputer pair built from standard components:\",\"(ii) A reimplementable selector--imputer design built from standard components:\")\n# method compared approaches\nrep(\"\\\\textbf{Proposed pair (frozen for the sealed evaluation).} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\\\ref{sec:impl}.\",\n\"\\\\textbf{Proposed pair.} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\\\ref{sec:impl}, selected on the development partition as the best-scoring configuration. The sealed evaluation ran either this pair or its latent-skill extension (below); the two have identical development scores, the recorded hash of the evaluated source tree matches neither retained source copy, and the available evidence cannot say which ran. Statements about the ``evaluated pair'' therefore refer to this selector--imputer family.\")\nrep(\"Table~\\\\ref{tab:final}\",  \"Table~\\\\ref{tab:final}\") if False else None\nrep(\"Sealed final metrics of the frozen pair (mean\",\"Sealed final metrics of the evaluated selector--imputer pair (mean\")\nrep(\"the proposed pair reaches $\\\\nrmse=0.562$\",\"the evaluated pair reaches $\\\\nrmse=0.562$\")\nrep(\"The sealed evaluation was run on the best-scoring configuration; the exact tie means the retained scores alone cannot distinguish the two sources (Supplementary Information).\",\n\"The sealed evaluation was run on one of these two configurations; the exact tie means the retained scores cannot say which (Supplementary Information).\")\nrep(\"evaluated a rank-Gauss, EM-Gaussian, wrapper-selected, NNLS-stacked pair on three leaderboards\",\"evaluated a rank-Gauss, EM-Gaussian, wrapper-selected, NNLS-stacked selector--imputer family (one member of it, not identifiable from the retained evidence) on three leaderboards\")\nrep(\"(4) \\\\emph{Configuration freezing.} The pair was chosen on the development partition and then evaluated once on the sealed partitions; the sealed numbers are not selected on, but\",\n\"(4) \\\\emph{Configuration identity and freezing.} The configuration was chosen on the development partition before the sealed evaluation, but no artifact records which of the two tied configurations the sealed run used or that it was run only once; the sealed numbers are not selected on, but\")\n# SI\nrep(\"they are read from the entry-point source of the evaluated configuration (\\\\texttt{src/program.py}: the EM, greedy-selection, wrapper-selection, stacking and prediction routines).\",\n\"they are read from the entry-point source of the proposed-pair source copy (\\\\texttt{src/program.py}: the EM, greedy-selection, wrapper-selection, stacking and prediction routines). This copy is not verified to be the exact tree evaluated in the sealed run (see below), so the constants describe the proposed pair, not a confirmed record of the sealed configuration.\")\nrep(\"Constants of the proposed pair.}\",\"Constants of the proposed pair, read from its source copy (not verified as the exact sealed-evaluated tree).}\")\nrep(\"The description in the main text is of the shared stack, and the extension is described here for completeness.\",\"The main text describes the shared stack, and the extension is described here because the sealed run may have used it.\")\nopen(p,'w').write(t)\nE\ngrep -n \"frozen\\|evaluated once\\|once on\" paper.tex; grep -n \"frozen\\|once\" claims_grounding.json | head"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2387,"cache_read_input_tokens":80377,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2387},"output_tokens":35,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52aaa56e-e9a6-4eb1-bcf5-1fba4e8e3ca8","uuid":"c89a486e-2456-4475-a769-fe59b26d2dfa","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:01:35.665Z","request_id":"req_011CgwBdmnFapEQABG6mrfoo"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AKgEnYbvNe7suNiDghBV6s","type":"tool_result","content":"88:If benchmark scores are jointly Gaussian with covariance $\\Sigma$ and benchmarks in $S$ are observed, the conditional mean $\\mathbb{E}[y_U\\mid y_S]$ ($U$ the unrun benchmarks) minimises mean squared error, and the expected squared error summed over $U$ equals $\\operatorname{tr}\\Sigma_{U\\mid S}$. Choosing $S$ greedily to reduce this trace (an A-optimal design) is therefore a principled selector \\emph{under the Gaussian model}. Concretely, for a row-vector of scores $y\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\Sigma)$ and observation noise $\\lambda\\ge 0$, the posterior covariance after observing benchmark $j$ is $R'=R-R_{\\cdot j}R_{j\\cdot}/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$ with $R$ initialised at $\\Sigma$, and the resulting drop of the summed variance over the remaining benchmarks is $\\sum_{i\\in U,\\,i\\neq j}R_{ij}^2/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$. A benchmark scores highly if it is strongly correlated with many others and itself has substantial variance, so redundant benchmarks are avoided once one of them has been chosen, because the update deflates their shared variance. The classical near-optimality guarantees for greedy selection \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor} apply to submodular objectives; we do not claim that the trace criterion with noise and missing data satisfies the required conditions here. We state the Gaussian argument only as motivation: we did not check it against a controlled experiment, and real score matrices are bounded, skewed and sparsely observed, which can make the trace criterion misaligned with realised held-out error. Three design choices address this misalignment:","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52aaa56e-e9a6-4eb1-bcf5-1fba4e8e3ca8","uuid":"946dd3f0-c465-4e89-8899-9a44e70d7e5d","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:01:35.767Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"88:If benchmark scores are jointly Gaussian with covariance $\\Sigma$ and benchmarks in $S$ are observed, the conditional mean $\\mathbb{E}[y_U\\mid y_S]$ ($U$ the unrun benchmarks) minimises mean squared error, and the expected squared error summed over $U$ equals $\\operatorname{tr}\\Sigma_{U\\mid S}$. Choosing $S$ greedily to reduce this trace (an A-optimal design) is therefore a principled selector \\emph{under the Gaussian model}. Concretely, for a row-vector of scores $y\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\Sigma)$ and observation noise $\\lambda\\ge 0$, the posterior covariance after observing benchmark $j$ is $R'=R-R_{\\cdot j}R_{j\\cdot}/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$ with $R$ initialised at $\\Sigma$, and the resulting drop of the summed variance over the remaining benchmarks is $\\sum_{i\\in U,\\,i\\neq j}R_{ij}^2/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$. A benchmark scores highly if it is strongly correlated with many others and itself has substantial variance, so redundant benchmarks are avoided once one of them has been chosen, because the update deflates their shared variance. The classical near-optimality guarantees for greedy selection \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor} apply to submodular objectives; we do not claim that the trace criterion with noise and missing data satisfies the required conditions here. We state the Gaussian argument only as motivation: we did not check it against a controlled experiment, and real score matrices are bounded, skewed and sparsely observed, which can make the trace criterion misaligned with realised held-out error. Three design choices address this misalignment:","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
